  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## Summary (2026-10-12)\n\nAll two-block BPS configurations (6 GL(4) orbits of the polarisation pair × every bridge length ℓ × bridge starting on A or F; 180 configurations at L = 2, 3, 4 pairs), whole multiplets probed by random global rotations:\n\n- **No configuration has an LR selection rule that persists.** \"Only LR states couple\" happens only at L = 2 (orbit both-zero, ℓ = 2, one LR state). At L = 3 and L = 4 every configuration that couples to any non-BPS LR state also couples to non-LR states.\n- The **generic orbit is the note's configuration** (any two BPS blocks with a₁·f₂ ≠ 0 ≠ a₂·f₁ are GL(4)-equivalent to (e₁,e₄) | (e₁−e₄, e₄+e₁)), so changing polarisations within it cannot create a rule.\n- The opposite happens often: \"only non-LR states couple\" in 8, 14, 24 configurations at L = 2, 3, 4 (e.g. generic ℓ = 1, 2L−1 at L = 2, 4; half-degenerate orbits at specific ℓ), but the pattern is not uniform in L (generic ℓ = 1 is \"both\" at L = 3).\n- Caveat: probing multiplets by random rotations makes p ≠ r and q ≠ 0 states reachable, which the note's HW-projected table did not see; \"non-LR\" includes those.\n\nInterpretation: in 2603 the rule comes from ⟨W|(t₊ − t₋) = 0 for the BPS bra plus the bridge operator being built from transfer matrices. For ABJM, single globally rotated BPS blocks never give such a bra here; an LR rule would need a bra annihilated by t̄₁(u) − t̄₃(u+h), which no two-block BPS bra is (at L = 3, 4).",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ],
